          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#definition"} Definition
              %li
                %a{:href => "#properties"} Properties
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#relation_to_schemes"} Relation to schemes
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+geometry"} algebraic geometry
            %strong algebraic variety
            (not to be confused with
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variety+of+algebras"} variety of algebras
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scheme"} scheme
            which is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral+scheme"} integral
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/separated+scheme"} separated
            and of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+type"} finite type
            over an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraically+closed+field"} algebraically closed field
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
          %p
            Classically, the term algebraic variety referred to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scheme"} scheme
            as above which is further
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-projective+scheme"} quasi-projective
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+space"} projective space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+space"} projective space
            as the geometric locus of zeros of a set of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polynomial"} polynomial
            equations in finitely many variables. (The first example of an algebraic variety which is not quasi-projective was given by
            = succeed ".)" do
              %span.newWikiWord
                Nagata
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Nagata"} ?
          %p
            Historically, there were several formalisms of various schools including the Italian school of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+geometry"} algebraic geometry
            in the early 20th century (Veronese, Castelnuovo, Severi, …), the American school between the two wars (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Oscar+Zariski"}> Oscar Zariski
            ),
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andre+Weil"} Andre Weil
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-Pierre+Serre"} Jean-Pierre Serre
            and finally the language of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/schemes"} schemes
            introduced by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck"} Grothendieck
            school. One should note that in the case of (esp.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+variety"} projective
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            Given an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraically+closed+field"} algebraically closed field
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %strong
              algebraic
              = succeed "-variety" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            usually means either a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasiprojective+variety"} quasiprojective variety
            or an abstract variety (in the sense of Serre). ‘Quasiprojective’ unifies affine, quasiaffine,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+variety"} projective
            and embedded quasiprojective
            = succeed "-varieties." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                An embedded affine
                = succeed "-variety" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msubsup
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi A
                          %mi k
                          %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{A}^n_k
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nullstellensatz"} Nullstellensatz
                there is a more invariant definition.
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/affine+variety"} Affine
                = succeed "-varieties" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/maximal+spectrum"} maximal spectra
                (= sets of
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/maximal+ideals"} maximal ideals
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noetherian+ring"} noetherian
                (commutative unital)
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+algebra"} algebras
                without
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nilpotent+element"} nilpotents
                with the
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Zariski+topology"} Zariski topology
              %p
                Affine varietes can be embedded as closed subvarieties into an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/affine+space"} affine space
                (in the sense of algebraic geometry). As topological spaces affine varieties are
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noetherian+space"} noetherian
            %li
              %p
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+variety"} Projective
                = succeed "-varieties" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graded+algebra"} graded
                = succeed "-algebras," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msubsup
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi P
                          %mi k
                          %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{P}^n_k
            %li
              %p
                Embedded
                %strong quasiaffine
                = succeed "-varieties" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
                = succeed "-varieties." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %li
              %p
                Embedded
                %strong quasiprojective
                = succeed "-varieties" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
                = succeed "-varieties." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
                = succeed "s." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+ringed+space"} locally ringed space
              %p
                In fact, by noticing that the affine
                = succeed "-space" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
          %p
            Morphisms between varieties are sometimes called
            = succeed "." do
              %span.newWikiWord
                regular maps
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/regular+maps"} ?
          %p
            Sometimes a smooth algebraic variety may also be called
            = succeed "." do
              %strong algebraic manifold
          %p
            An abstract
            = succeed "-prevariety" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            = succeed "-variety." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            = succeed "-prevarieties" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            = succeed "-varieties." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\to X\to X
            = succeed "-variety" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
          %p
            Morphisms of algebraic varieties are what is also called
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+map"} regular map
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#relation_to_schemes Relation to schemes
          %p
            There is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence of categories
            between the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral+schemes"} integral schemes
            of finite type over
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Spec
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spec\,k
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            is an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraically+closed+field"} algebraically closed field
            = succeed "-varieties." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
          %p
            Of course, given a variety the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scheme"} scheme
            and variety have different sets of points; the points in common are the closed points of the scheme. The remaining points are the generic points of subvarieties. Generic points were often used, without proper foundations, in other language, already in the works of the Italian school.
          %p
            Some modern algebraic geometers mean, by varieties, objects of certain slightly bigger categories of relative
            = succeed "-schemes" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            is not necessarily
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Spec
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spec\,k
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            a field); typically they are required to be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/separated+scheme"} separated
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reduced+scheme"} reduced
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relative+scheme"} schemes
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism+of+finite+type"} of finite type
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+point"} geometric point
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+point+of+an+algebraic+variety"} singular point of an algebraic variety
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+algebraic+variety"} complete algebraic variety
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semialgebraic+manifold"} semialgebraic manifold
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li
              Igor Shafarevich,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Basic algebraic geometry
            %li
              J. S. Milne,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Algebraic geometry
              %a{:href => "http://www.jmilne.org/math/CourseNotes/AG.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              Joe Harris,
              %em Introductory algebraic geometry
            %li
              chapter I of Robin Hartshorne,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Algebraic geometry
          %p
            An amusing discussion on the differences between schemes and varieties can be found at
            = succeed ":" do
              %em Secret blogging seminar
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://sbseminar.wordpress.com/2009/08/06/algebraic-geometry-without-prime-ideals"} algebraic geometry without prime ideals
